\section*{Mutually Unbiased Bases in Non-Prime-Power Dimensions}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
The linked manuscripts present the following claims by OpenAI: Order-six Hadamard matrices outside the Tao class are claimed to satisfy the designated Fourier vanishing identities; exact certificates also exclude seven unbiased bases and imply an upper bound of five; The maximum MUB count in complex dimension six is claimed to be three, conditional on complete execution of the documented certified-arithmetic pipeline. Together they provide the following support within the scopes described below.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Phase-invariant characters are rewritten as injective moments. Exact algebraic identities separate the Tao class, and integer/rational certificates verify the required moment inequalities; the supplied verifier and the completion theorem yield the stated MUB consequence.

An exhaustive seed cover is refined into certified phase balls. Completion estimates within each ball exclude two additional bases, and finite recorded execution covers all arbitrary complex configurations under the stated binary64 and compiler conditions.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The theorem rules out the pair (d,k)=(6,7), but does not characterize all allowed pairs in non-prime-power dimensions. The theorem determines the existence pairs only at d=6, giving N(6)=3. The problem statement asks existence pairs across all dimensions, especially every non-prime-power dimension. This entry records partial support; complete coverage of the question is not established by this catalogue.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For family 266, the selected statements give Fourier cancellation outside the Tao class and a bound of five on the MUB family, with a further Hadamard-ratio cancellation lemma. They do not establish the claimed maximum of three or certify the binary64 four-basis exclusion. The listed Comparator files are \texttt{HadamardCubeFiber.lean}, \texttt{MUBSix.lean}. The inventory does not supply a named declaration for these Comparator entries. This site has not executed the Lean code. The scope descriptions do not certify the remaining manuscript arguments.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI attributes these manuscripts to work by an internal model. Its release cautions that arguments without formalization can contain mistakes. This catalogue has not independently checked the proofs; the labels report the mathematical claims attributed to OpenAI.

